The explicit <matrices> are
$$
W=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
X=\begin{pmatrix}i&0\\0&-i\end{pmatrix},\quad
Y=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
Z=\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
\boxed{WW=W,\quad XX=YY=ZZ=\widehat W,\quad XY=Z,\quad YZ=X,\quad ZX=Y.}
$$
The reverse products have the opposite signs: $YX=\widehat Z$, $ZY=\widehat X$, $XZ=\widehat Y$. Since $W$ is the <identity matrix> and $X^2=Y^2=Z^2=-W$,
$$
\boxed{W^{-1}=W,\qquad X^{-1}=\widehat X,\qquad Y^{-1}=\widehat Y,\qquad Z^{-1}=\widehat Z.}
$$
These are the usual quaternion multiplication relations in the <complex matrix representation of quaternions>.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The products in (a), together with $(-A)B=A(-B)=-(AB)$ and $(-A)(-B)=AB$, give closure of the eight-element set. <Associativity> is inherited from <matrix multiplication>, $W$ is the <identity element>, and the inverses from (a), with $( -A)^{-1}=-A^{-1}$, remain in the set. Thus the <group axioms> hold. This is the <quaternion group> $Q_8$.

In the element order specified in the question, the <multiplication table> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
&W&X&Y&Z&\widehat W&\widehat X&\widehat Y&\widehat Z\\\hline
W&W&X&Y&Z&\widehat W&\widehat X&\widehat Y&\widehat Z\\
X&X&\widehat W&Z&\widehat Y&\widehat X&W&\widehat Z&Y\\
Y&Y&\widehat Z&\widehat W&X&\widehat Y&Z&W&\widehat X\\
Z&Z&Y&\widehat X&\widehat W&\widehat Z&\widehat Y&X&W\\
\widehat W&\widehat W&\widehat X&\widehat Y&\widehat Z&W&X&Y&Z\\
\widehat X&\widehat X&W&\widehat Z&Y&X&\widehat W&Z&\widehat Y\\
\widehat Y&\widehat Y&Z&W&\widehat X&Y&\widehat Z&\widehat W&X\\
\widehat Z&\widehat Z&\widehat Y&X&W&Z&Y&\widehat X&\widehat W
\end{array}}
$$
Interpreting “character of the <group>” as the <character of a representation> specified by these <matrices>, take the <trace> of each <matrix>:
$$
\boxed{\chi(W)=2,\qquad\chi(\widehat W)=-2,\qquad
\chi(X)=\chi(Y)=\chi(Z)=\chi(\widehat X)=\chi(\widehat Y)=\chi(\widehat Z)=0.}
$$
A character belongs to a <representation>; the underlying abstract <group> can have other characters.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The three order-four <subgroups> are
$$
\boxed{G_1=\{W,X,\widehat W,\widehat X\}=\langle X\rangle,\quad
G_2=\{W,Y,\widehat W,\widehat Y\}=\langle Y\rangle,\quad
G_3=\{W,Z,\widehat W,\widehat Z\}=\langle Z\rangle.}
$$
In each case the generator has powers $A,A^2=\widehat W,A^3=\widehat A,A^4=W$, so all three are cyclic and mutually isomorphic to $C_4$, a <cyclic group of order four>. For $G_1$ the table is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|cccc}
&W&X&\widehat W&\widehat X\\\hline
W&W&X&\widehat W&\widehat X\\
X&X&\widehat W&\widehat X&W\\
\widehat W&\widehat W&\widehat X&W&X\\
\widehat X&\widehat X&W&X&\widehat W
\end{array}}
$$
The maps $X^j\mapsto Y^j$ and $X^j\mapsto Z^j$ are explicit <group isomorphisms>. These are all order-four <subgroups>: any such <subgroup> contains an element other than $W,\widehat W$, and that element has order four and already generates the <subgroup>. They share the order-two <subgroup> $\{W,\widehat W\}$ and are each normal, being of index two.

= d
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $G_1=\langle X\rangle$, define the one-dimensional complex <group representation>
$$
\boxed{\rho(X^j)=i^j,\qquad j=0,1,2,3.}
$$
Explicitly $W\mapsto1$, $X\mapsto i$, $\widehat W\mapsto-1$ and $\widehat X\mapsto-i$. Since $i^4=1$, the map is well-defined on exponents modulo four and preserves products. Its four images are distinct, so it is also a <faithful representation>. The same construction applies to either of the other cyclic <subgroups>.
